1
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:45,900
CONVERSATION 1
Welcome to Money Matters, a weekly program that helps you manage your money.

2
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:50,440
Tonight, I'll be talking to Mary Johnson about budgeting.

3
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:52,060
Hello everyone.

4
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:53,680
There's a magic about money.

5
00:00:53,680 --> 00:01:00,360
When it's not planned for, tracked, and kept a record of, it literally disappears.

6
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:04,860
What are some of the steps we can take to prevent this from happening?

7
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:07,780
It's all about keeping track of your money.

8
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:14,580
If you don't do that, you'll never be able to set any goals for your budget or have the discipline to stick to them.

9
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:17,120
That's easier said than done.

10
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:22,480
I read recently that only 41% of Americans adhere to a budget.

11
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:30,940
Yes, but knowing what you earn and what you spend can give you reassurance that you won't get into debt in the first place.

12
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:37,720
You can do this by adding up all of your sources of income you have and writing them all down on a piece of paper.

13
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:41,960
On the same page, write down all of your monthly expenses.

14
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:54,820
I'm always amazed at how much my expenses add up, but designating each item as an income or an expense really helps me have a much better sense of all my spending.

15
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:55,760
Right.

16
00:01:55,760 --> 00:02:03,760
Most people have no idea how much they spend each day, let alone each week or month, no matter how careful they are.

17
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,420
Next, subtract your monthly expenses from income.

18
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,280
If the result is positive, you are living within your means.

19
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:15,880
If the result is a negative number, you're going to have to cut back on your spending.

20
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:19,080
I'm usually a negative number.

21
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:23,800
I just can't resist the lure of all those prestigious goods.

22
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,900
Well, it's not a catastrophe.

23
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:28,780
But you do have to make some changes.

24
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:39,420
Try cutting back on those non-essential items, sell some stuff in your attic or shop online to avoid unnecessary temptations like chocolate.

25
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:50,540
Failing that you can always find yourself a part-time job.

26
00:02:50,540 --> 00:03:08,040
Q1. What does the man say about the weekly program?

27
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:24,240
Q2. What did the man read recently?

28
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:46,757
Q3. What does the woman suggest the man do first to avoid getting into debt?

29
00:03:46,757 --> 00:04:03,400
Q4. What does the woman say about online shopping?

30
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:04,700
CONVERSATION 2
Hi there.

31
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:06,620
I've come to see the flat.

32
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:08,420
My name is Mark Adams.

33
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:10,460
We spoke on the phone on Wednesday.

34
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:11,340
Hi Mark.

35
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:12,300
Come on up.

36
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:13,840
I'll buzz you in.

37
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:19,320
Green door on the second floor, on the right side.

38
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:20,840
Nice to meet you.

39
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:23,840
I spoke to all your references and they all checked out.

40
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:24,920
Okay?

41
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:26,140
So let me show you around.

42
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:31,500
The place actually belongs to my mother, but her health isn't great.

43
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:36,760
We finally managed to persuade her to move in with us and rent this old place out.

44
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:41,740
It's a great size, plenty of space, very versatile.

45
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:43,920
I think it's a winner for us.

46
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:44,600
Yes.

47
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,780
All the appliances are brand new.

48
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:51,200
There's a washing machine and a tumble dryer in the utility room next to the kitchen.

49
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:55,820
Lots of closet space, too, which is fabulous.

50
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:59,240
My wife has a ridiculous number of shoes.

51
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:04,540
Now, the big question, what about noise and the neighbors?

52
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:08,300
All the neighbors are elderly, so no noisy kids.

53
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:12,300
And the back of the house overlooks a clear and peaceful pond.

54
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:15,340
It's perfect if tranquility is what you are looking for.

55
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:17,540
That's good news.

56
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:25,980
We've been living in a less than glamorous part of Aberdeen, constantly harassed day and night by noisy neighbors.

57
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:28,200
Getting to work was a nightmare, too.

58
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:34,700
As we only have one car, and my wife has to use it as she works nights at the hospital.

59
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:40,660
If you like the place, it's yours as soon as I get a contract drawn up with the solicitor.

60
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:50,300
The first month's rent and a deposit are mandatory on signing the contract, then we can work out when is the best day for you to pay rent each month.

61
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:53,980
We'll be incredibly happy to be your new tenants.

62
00:05:53,980 --> 00:05:56,020
Thank you so much.

63
00:05:56,020 --> 00:06:12,700
My wife will be thrilled to get out of the shabby place we are now in and start filling those wardrobes with all those shoes.

64
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:30,160
Q5. What does the woman say about the flat?

65
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:49,440
Q6. What is the man's chief consideration in looking for a flat?

66
00:06:49,440 --> 00:07:08,100
Q7. What does the man have to do on signing the contract?

67
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:57,820
Q8. Why does the man say his wife will feel very excited if they move into the flat?

68
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:07,660
PASSAGE 1
A new study has found a positive correlation between how much television children watch and their parents' stress levels.

69
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:08,340
Why?

70
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:13,940
Because the more television kids watch, the more they're exposed to advertising.

71
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:21,460
The more advertising they see, the more likely they are to insist on purchasing items when they go with their parents to the store.

72
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:26,540
This could generate conflict if the parents refuse.

73
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:32,480
All that, researchers say, can contribute to parents' overall stress levels.

74
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:34,220
What's the solution?

75
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:38,600
Perhaps the most obvious is curtailing screen time.

76
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:43,700
Commercial content is there for a reason: to elicit purchasing behavior.

77
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:47,660
So, parents might want to shut off the TV.

78
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:54,160
Researchers concede that this is easier said than done, so they suggest another option.

79
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:58,900
Parents can change how they talk to their kids about purchases.

80
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:05,460
The researchers suggest that parents seek input from their children on family purchasing decisions.

81
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:08,580
They shouldn't try to control all purchases.

82
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:15,900
Instead, parents might tell their children things like, "I will listen to your advice on certain products or brands."

83
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:23,980
This type of communication, the researchers assert, can lead to children making fewer purchasing demands.

84
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:27,080
That means less parents' stress.

85
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:35,140
However, the protective effect of this kind of communication diminishes with greater exposure to television.

86
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:39,500
This is because advertising aimed at children is especially persuasive.

87
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:50,200
Advertisers use an assortment of tactics, such as bright colors, happy music, and celebrity endorsements to appeal to children.

88
00:09:50,200 --> 00:10:08,380
Plus, children don't have the cognitive ability to fully understand advertising's intent, which makes them particularly vulnerable to advertisements.

89
00:10:08,380 --> 00:10:27,400
Q9. What has the new study found about children watching television?

90
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:48,000
Q10. What are parents advised to do to reduce the impact of TV commercials?

91
00:10:48,000 --> 00:11:08,040
Q11. What makes children particularly vulnerable to TV commercials?

92
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:10,860
PASSAGE 2
Everyone is supposed to cheer for good guys.

93
00:11:10,860 --> 00:11:13,660
We should only punish the bad guys.

94
00:11:13,660 --> 00:11:15,360
That's not what we always do.

95
00:11:15,360 --> 00:11:20,200
Most of the time, we do indeed reward good people.

96
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:26,840
We also often punish people who harm others, or who aren't good team players.

97
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:35,380
But sometimes the good guys also get punished or criticized, specifically because they are so good.

98
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:40,040
This seems baffling because it's detrimental to group cooperation.

99
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:45,600
However, the phenomenon has been discovered in multiple fields.

100
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:48,460
It has been found in every society.

101
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:50,300
Why does this happen?

102
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:52,660
Research suggests a simple reason.

103
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:57,780
When one person looks really good, others look bad by comparison.

104
00:11:57,780 --> 00:12:06,200
Those others then have an incentive in stopping that person from looking good, especially if they can't or won't compete.

105
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:11,260
After all, we're all judged in comparison with others.

106
00:12:11,260 --> 00:12:15,560
When faced with someone better, what can a normal person do?

107
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:18,840
One option is to actively compete.

108
00:12:18,840 --> 00:12:22,000
A second option is to bring that person down.

109
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:32,420
That is to suppress their cooperation or work ethic, infer selfish motives for their actions, or imply real or imagined hypocrisy.

110
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:39,740
Other tactics include attacking them on unrelated dimensions or punishing them outright.

111
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:41,520
Why does this matter?

112
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:49,660
Critics often attack the motives of people who protect the environment, donate money, or work too hard.

113
00:12:49,660 --> 00:12:56,300
Such good deeds are dismissed as naive or hypocritical by those who do not perform those deeds.

114
00:12:56,300 --> 00:13:03,160
This criticism may ultimately discourage people from doing good deeds.

115
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:15,240
So it's important to recognize these attacks for what they are.

116
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:34,620
Q12. What baffling phenomenon is discussed in the passage?

117
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:52,860
Q13. How are we all judged according to the passage?

118
00:13:52,860 --> 00:14:12,080
Q14. What can a normal person do when faced with people who perform better?

119
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:59,600
Q15. What may discourage people from performing good deeds?

120
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:11,340
RECORDING 1
In America, most researchers concede that boys and girls are brought up in different ways, taught different skills and rewarded for different acts.

121
00:15:11,340 --> 00:15:16,640
Women, it is agreed, excel at certain tasks, men at others.

122
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:23,100
There is little argument that some personality traits appear more dominant in one sex than in the other.

123
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:29,120
All of this notwithstanding, gender differences are very much in the media these days.

124
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:35,480
Since the rise of the women's movement, gender role behavior has come under closer scrutiny.

125
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:37,820
How has this affected friendship?

126
00:15:37,820 --> 00:15:41,840
How do the sexes differ in their friendship relations?

127
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:49,000
Most pre-teen children have a best friend who is usually someone of the same sex and similar age.

128
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:54,920
Both sexes share an essentially positive recollection of these childhood friendships.

129
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:57,640
They do not differ in this respect.

130
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:05,520
However, the type of play engaged in during these early friendships is telling of the difference to come.

131
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:09,660
Boys tend to form play groups that are competitive in nature.

132
00:16:09,660 --> 00:16:14,500
Girls' groups more frequently revolve around cooperative enterprises.

133
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:29,980
Thus at an early age, boys become concerned with trying hard and winning, while girls, by contrast, play house and school, engaging in roles that require complementary support.

134
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:36,700
Men recall being highly responsive to and aware of the gender role opinions of other boys.

135
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:42,440
Girls in pre-teen years appear to be less susceptible to gender role pressure.

136
00:16:42,440 --> 00:16:48,780
It is not until the dating years that women report being concerned with feminine behavior.

137
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:56,020
Males, for the most part, are responsive to the suggestion that their behavior is unmanly at almost any age.

138
00:16:56,020 --> 00:17:06,160
These early attitudes reinforced by social conditioning continue to play an active part in the friendships of both sexes during adolescence.

139
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:13,000
This is a period when the majority of males, once again, report a close alliance with same-sex friends.

140
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:25,220
Now, however, with heightened intensity, considerable energy is devoted to competing for position, and a definite undercurrent of competition permeates the relationship.

141
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:32,120
Although in dissimilar fashion, females share equally fragile relationships at this age.

142
00:17:32,120 --> 00:17:37,760
For them, the bond of loyalty extends only to the line of romantic involvement.

143
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:47,360
This is most apt to be the case in late adolescence, when dating and relationships with boys take sharp precedence over sisterhood.

144
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:53,560
Actually, dating dilutes the intensity of same-sex friendships for men also.

145
00:17:53,560 --> 00:18:08,580
For the majority of us, the moment we begin to date seriously, there's a competition between romance and friendship.

146
00:18:08,580 --> 00:18:27,220
Q16. What does the speaker say about most pre-teen children?

147
00:18:27,220 --> 00:18:47,240
Q17. What do most males devote much of their energy to during adolescence?

148
00:18:47,240 --> 00:19:05,240
Q18. What do children do when they reach late adolescence?

149
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:07,760
RECORDING 2
Good afternoon.

150
00:19:07,760 --> 00:19:15,460
Today's lecture, we'll be talking about how and when to disclose a disability when applying for a job.

151
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:21,580
On average, about 20% of the population has some form of disability.

152
00:19:21,580 --> 00:19:27,840
Most countries these days have equal opportunity and nondiscrimination laws.

153
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:38,960
Yet, disabled people often find it hard to decide when, how, and if at all, to raise their disability problem with a potential employer.

154
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:43,000
There is uncertainty about how a recruiter will perceive their disability.

155
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:52,720
As such, many candidates fear they wouldn't be considered for a position as a result of disclosing this personal information.

156
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:59,240
And research has validated this as a genuine concern for many job applicants.

157
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:04,560
It's a natural reaction, but it shouldn't be a reason to stay quiet.

158
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:12,840
People need to remember that they are applying for a position they have the skills and experience to excel in.

159
00:20:12,840 --> 00:20:21,080
Discussing a disability with a potential employer may help them make reasonable workplace adjustments in their favor.

160
00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:27,220
It's most appropriate to discuss a disability when they reply to confirm an interview.

161
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:37,160
This information needn't be put up front in their cover letter or resume, because it's probably not relevant to the position itself.

162
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:47,300
Candidates with disabilities should feel they have the power to make their own decisions around sharing this information free from prejudice.

163
00:20:47,300 --> 00:20:56,480
If they find an organization that doesn't celebrate diversity and inclusion, it could say a lot about the company's culture.

164
00:20:56,480 --> 00:21:00,220
Perhaps the organization isn't the right fit.

165
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:12,400
It's important for them to remember that they are seeking a manager and employer that's going to be supportive and continue to give them a great employment experience.

166
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:23,820
Companies sometimes offer candidates the chance to disclose disabilities on their application form, but people shouldn't feel restricted by this method or timing.

167
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:28,300
People should avoid sharing the name of their disability or condition.

168
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:36,060
There is always the risk that the recruiter will research information that is inaccurate or irrelevant.

169
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:58,400
If people don't think their disability will impact their ability to perform in the advertised position, then it's entirely their entitlement to choose when and whether to share this information.

170
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:19,040
Q19. Why do disabled job applicants feel reluctant to disclose their disability information?

171
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:40,160
Q20. When does the speaker suggest applicants reveal their disability information?

172
00:22:40,160 --> 00:23:00,500
Q21. What are people advised to do when filling out their job application form?

173
00:23:00,500 --> 00:23:08,780
RECORDING 3
Smartphones distract attention and reduce learning because of their potential to offer activities more inviting than study.

174
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:11,780
But what about background sound alone?

175
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:24,280
A group of American researchers compared students' comprehension of verbal material when reading in the presence of background speech, instrumental music or general noise.

176
00:23:24,280 --> 00:23:31,400
General noise is neutral, such as that from the sound of an air conditioner or fan.

177
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:36,520
Students' scores were most depressed in the presence of background speech.

178
00:23:36,520 --> 00:23:41,280
Comprehension was slightly better with the presence of music than with speech.

179
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:50,080
However, when they were asked to identify melodies rather than understand text, background music interfered more.

180
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:58,840
When the background speech was in a language unfamiliar to participants, there was little if any hindrance of reading comprehension.

181
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:11,260
British researchers compared the effects of background speech, vocal music, instrumental music, general background noise, and silence on short-term memory.

182
00:24:11,260 --> 00:24:14,940
Background speech had the biggest negative effect.

183
00:24:14,940 --> 00:24:19,680
Vocal music was slightly more disruptive than instrumental.

184
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:24,360
In general, background noise and silence were least disruptive.

185
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:35,760
It seems the degree of interference from background noise depends on the overlap between the processing required on the task and the processing required to screen out the background noise.

186
00:24:35,760 --> 00:24:45,400
The studies suggest that when people read and when they try to remember any verbal material, background speech will inhibit their ability.

187
00:24:45,400 --> 00:24:49,320
Instrumental music will have, at worst, a slight effect.

188
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:58,140
When students write essays, however, other research has found it is best to reduce all background noise as much as possible.

189
00:24:58,140 --> 00:25:01,960
Not everyone reacts in the same way to distractions.

190
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:06,860
Other studies suggest some aspects of personality may make a difference.

191
00:25:06,860 --> 00:25:20,560
The researchers subjected shy, quiet people and confident, outgoing ones to high arousal or low arousal background music, general noise or silence while asking them to remember words.

192
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:30,640
Everyone performed best in the silent condition, but less sociable people were more negatively affected by each of the distractions.

193
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:52,600
So when children are reading and trying to incorporate new material, parents could consider allowing some background music, particularly if it is instrumental and their child is the outgoing type.

194
00:25:52,600 --> 00:26:15,260
Q22. What did the American researchers find about students' reading comprehension?

195
00:26:15,260 --> 00:26:58,380
Q23. What determines the degree of interference from background noise according to the passage?

196
00:26:58,380 --> 00:26:58,430
Q24. What do we learn about the effect of distractions on people of different personalities?

197
00:26:58,430 --> 00:27:22,104
Q25. What does the speaker suggest parents do when their children are reading?
